# Vendored dependencies - the pin record.
#
# Format: <name> <upstream-commit-or-tag> <license-id> <upstream-url>
#
# One uncommented line per vendor/<name>/ tree, and `<name>` must equal the
# directory name. The four fields are machine-read by generate_release_sbom.rb
# (`parse_vendor_pins`) and by ai_package_gate.rb; everything else on this page
# is a comment, including the per-pin detail blocks. A commented line is NOT a
# pin, deliberately - an intended-but-not-yet-imported dependency must not be
# mistaken for shipped bytes.
#
# Sources are COMMITTED, not fetched at build time. SPM source targets need the
# files present in the mirror, and a package whose build reaches the network is
# a package that stops building the day a host moves.
#
# PERMISSIVE ONLY, as in the AI package: an Apache-2.0 package whose promise is
# unrestricted commercial use by the apps that statically link it can never
# carry copyleft. ai_package_gate.rb checks the licence id below against the
# schema's copyleft dimension.

## sqlite  -  vendor/sqlite/
#
# Release          3.53.4
# Source id        2026-07-24 19:02:57
#                  bf7c7f30031888f4e796e429ab3978879485813aaca6f641c7b33e4e09459bcc
# Artifact         https://sqlite.org/2026/sqlite-amalgamation-3530400.zip
# SHA3-256         628a44cfe82c66aed1ccbbe85a562d2e33ebe64b3288981ed76285612227934e
#                  (sqlite.org publishes SHA3-256, not SHA-256, for its downloads;
#                   the SHA-256 of the same zip is
#                   1e71ddf93849c6a6ecf58b827c0692073d2dd7ee40196158068f7b29f422e87d)
# License          public domain. SQLite ships no LICENSE file; the author's
#                  disclaimer and blessing are carried in every source file's
#                  header and reproduced in vendor/sqlite/LICENSE.
# Pruned to        sqlite3.c, sqlite3.h, sqlite3ext.h. shell.c (the CLI) is not
#                  vendored - a library has no business carrying a REPL.
#
# WHERE IT IS USED, and where it deliberately is not:
#
#   - The CMake lane (Android AAR, the desktop JNI natives, the local C++ gate)
#     compiles this amalgamation INTO libdespia_local with hidden visibility.
#     Android has no NDK-public SQLite, so a bundled copy is not a preference,
#     it is the only option.
#   - The Apple lane now compiles THIS file too. It used to link the platform's
#     libsqlite3 instead, on the reasoning that iOS always ships SQLite and a
#     second static copy beside a sync vendor's own is a duplicate-symbol
#     argument nobody wins. That reasoning was sound about symbols and wrong
#     about everything else: the platform SQLite has no sqlite-vec, so the Apple
#     lane had no vectors at all, and the binding above it was a SECOND
#     implementation of the store semantics that the 26 fixtures never ran
#     against. Two implementations drift; that is what fixtures exist to catch,
#     and they could only see one of them.
#
#     The duplicate-symbol concern is answered rather than ignored: SQLITE_API
#     decoration hides every sqlite3_* entry point on the Apple build too, so a
#     sync vendor's own copy does not collide. vec_* is NOT hidden there, because
#     SPM forbids the `unsafeFlags` that would do it - Package.swift states that
#     limit exactly.
#
#   The consequence is now the good one: both lanes run the SAME SQLite build and
#   the same vector extension, and the 26 cases in OpenSource/Conformance/base
#   run against one implementation instead of holding two apart.
#
# THE ANDROID RULE: the library exports NO sqlite3_* symbols. A Despia app can
# carry a sync vendor that ships its own SQLite (PowerSync does), and two
# libraries exporting the same symbols is a load-order coin flip - the vendor's
# calls can bind to our SQLite or ours to theirs, whichever the dynamic linker
# saw first. Enforced by, in order:
#
#   1. -fvisibility=hidden on every translation unit (CMake's
#      C_VISIBILITY_PRESET/CXX_VISIBILITY_PRESET), so nothing is exported that
#      was not asked to be;
#   2. -DSQLITE_API=__attribute__((visibility("hidden"))) - SQLite's own
#      supported build define, so the public API decorations are explicitly
#      hidden even if a lane forgets (1). This is a BUILD DEFINE: the vendored
#      source is never edited, because a patched amalgamation is a fork with a
#      CVE watch of its own;
#   3. -DSQLITE_VEC_STATIC, which collapses sqlite-vec's export decoration to
#      nothing for the same reason;
#   4. the empirical check - `nm -D --defined-only` over the built library must
#      list only the despia_local_* ABI. `ruby OpenSource/Local/tools/symbol_check.rb`
#      runs it and fails on any sqlite3_ or vec_ symbol.
#
# Hardening defines carried on both lanes, all of them SQLite's own:
#
#   SQLITE_DQS=0                     a double-quoted string is a string, never a
#                                    fallback identifier - the misspelled-column
#                                    class of bug becomes an error
#   SQLITE_THREADSAFE=1              serialized; one Base handle is reachable
#                                    from the module's queue and the JNI lane
#   SQLITE_DEFAULT_MEMSTATUS=0       drops the allocator's global counters
#   SQLITE_DEFAULT_WAL_SYNCHRONOUS=1 WAL + NORMAL, the phone default
#   SQLITE_LIKE_DOESNT_MATCH_BLOBS
#   SQLITE_MAX_EXPR_DEPTH=0
#   SQLITE_OMIT_DEPRECATED
#   SQLITE_OMIT_LOAD_EXTENSION       nothing loads a .so into this database.
#                                    sqlite-vec is linked in and registered by
#                                    an explicit call, so the extension loader
#                                    is pure attack surface
#   SQLITE_OMIT_SHARED_CACHE
#   SQLITE_USE_ALLOCA
#   SQLITE_ENABLE_FTS5               full-text search: the other half of RAG,
#                                    and the reason a hybrid query needs no
#                                    second engine
#
# SQLITE_ENABLE_JSON1 is not passed: JSON has been built in since 3.38.
#
# The pin is the RELEASE, not a commit: sqlite.org is not the upstream VCS this
# tree came from - the amalgamation is a generated release artifact, and its
# release number is what a CVE feed and a bill of materials both key on.
sqlite      3.53.4  public-domain  https://sqlite.org

